            "text": "At dawn, the city seems to remember that it is more than traffic, deadlines, and illuminated windows. Before the buses begin their impatient groaning, a thin blue silence settles over the rooftops. In that silence, small things become enormous: the click of a kettle, the scrape of a chair, the first bird testing a note.\n\nI like this hour because it asks nothing. It does not demand productivity or optimism. It merely opens a door. Through it come possibilities that daylight often hides. A person might walk farther than usual, noticing a cracked statue, a sleeping dog, or a garden surviving behind a fence. Someone else might write a sentence that changes the shape of an entire day.\n\nWe tend to measure life by events, yet much of it is made from intervals: waiting for water to boil, standing beside a window, listening to rain finish its argument with the roof. These moments appear empty only when we hurry past them. Given attention, they become rooms with hidden furniture.\n\nPerhaps peace is not a grand destination but a skill of noticing. Perhaps wonder is less like lightning and more like a lamp left on in a hallway. We find it by returning, patiently, to what is already near.\n\nThen the city wakes fully. Engines start, doors close, messages arrive. Still, somewhere beneath the noise, that early silence remains, folded like a small letter in the pocket of the day, available whenever we remember to unfold it and read it again."
          }
        ],
